@ValWood I think that for fly and human these annotations are ok - from my notes ". At low or zero membrane potentials, the aqueous pore is in an open state, allowing the diffusion of small hydrophilic molecules with high conductance and anionic selectivity. At membrane potentials above 30-40mV the pore forms a closed state, but will still allow the passage of small ions with a low conductance and cationic selectivity. A voltage-sensitive domain controls gating."

So, I think that it can be regulated by membrane potential e.g. in apoptosis, but generally is just a pore.

Can't see any evidence in flies for tRNA transport. Some in plants PMID:17105808.
